<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Federal Charter of 1291</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1291">1291</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Bundesbrief</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Federal Charter of 1291 is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alliance document dated to early August 1291, in which the valley communities of Uri, Schwyz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidwalden">Nidwalden</a> promised each other aid against outside attack and agreed rules for keeping the peace and settling disputes. Rediscovered in the 18th century, it came to be treated as the founding document of the Swiss Confederation. It is kept in Schwyz, and 1 August is the Swiss National Day.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/federal-charter-of-1291.jpg" alt="Federal Charter of 1291" width="320" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesbrief.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Federal Charter, or Letter of Alliance, known in German as the Bundesbrief, records an Eternal Alliance of the League of the Three Forest Cantons, a union of Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden in what is now central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. It is written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and refers to an earlier pact that is lost or was never written down. It is one of a series of alliances from which the Old Swiss Confederacy emerged.</p><p>It was probably meant to ensure legal certainty after the death of Rudolf I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> on 15 July 1291. The parties are called conspirati and coniurati, traditionally translated in German as Eidgenossen and in English as confederates.</p><p>The first two paragraphs commit all three communities to the joint defence of the three valleys. The rest of the Charter concerns judicial matters. It calls for arbitration in conflicts, rejects foreign judges, sets the death penalty for murderers and exile for arsonists, and commands obedience to judges and their verdicts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Its authenticity was once disputed as a supposed modern forgery, but historians now agree that it is a product of the 14th century. In 1991 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parchment">parchment</a> was radiocarbon dated at ETH to between 1252 and 1312 with a probability of 85 per cent, or alternatively to between 1352 and 1385 with a probability of 15 per cent. It is therefore not a forgery tied to the founding of the federal state in 1848 and is better seen against Chapter 15 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a>, in which Charles IV outlawed leagues and conspiracies such as the city alliances. At the time agreements were mostly verbal, and documents drawn up later might have their contents or dates changed to suit current purposes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Charter became important in Swiss historiography only in the late 19th century. Before then the foundation of the Confederacy was traditionally dated to 1307, following Aegidius Tschudi, the year inscribed on the Tell Monument commissioned in 1895. The idea of 1291 as the founding date was first put forward in a report of the Federal Department of Home Affairs of 21 November 1889, for a combined celebration in 1891. A national holiday on 1 August was suggested in 1899 and made official only in 1994.</p><p>A Federal Charter Archive designed by Joseph Beeler was opened in Schwyz in 1936, and is now the Museum of the Swiss Charters of Confederation, where the Charter is exhibited.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Federal Charter of 1291” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Federal Charter of 1291</b> became the official founding document only in the 1890s, when 1 August was chosen as the Swiss national day.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In late medieval and early modern Europe the Old Swiss Confederacy grew from the <b>Federal Charter of 1291</b> among Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden into a web of pacts among thirteen cantons by 1513, whose delegates met in a diet, the Tagsatzung, and the Dutch Republic rested on the…</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1291 they joined Uri and Unterwalden in the <b>Federal Charter</b>, and in 1315 they defeated a Habsburg army at Morgarten, securing their independence and lending their name to the whole Swiss Confederacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-schwyz">Canton of Schwyz</a></li><li><blockquote>Nidwalden swore the <b>Federal Charter of 1291</b> with the Canton of Uri and the Canton of Schwyz, and Obwalden soon joined, so Unterwalden counted among the three original forest cantons of the Old Swiss Confederacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-unterwalden">Canton of Unterwalden</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1291 Uri joined Schwyz and Unterwalden in the <b>Federal Charter</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-uri">Canton of Uri</a></li></ul></section>
